A registered service that never answers must not hang its client forever.

THIS IS THE SINGLE REASON MONEYMONEY CANNOT OPEN, and the chain is measured end to end.

trustd blocks in mbr_uid_to_uuid before it can create its own XPC listener, so it never answers
anything. That call is a membership lookup libinfo sends to opendirectoryd over an xpc_pipe.
bootstrap_look_up SUCCEEDS because launchd knows the NAME whether or not the job is alive and
checked in, so the message sits on a port nobody is reading and the sender waits in mach_msg with no
deadline. Security then waits on trustd, forever, and the application sits on its splash.

On macOS these round trips have no timeout because launchd guarantees the service: the name resolves
only when something is there to serve it. That guarantee does not hold in this container.

A bounded wait turns the hang into an ERROR, and every caller on this path already handles one:
libinfo falls back to building a compatibility UUID in-process, and Security has its own no-trustd
path. MACH_RCV_TIMED_OUT is already mapped to EIO by handleMachMessageReceiveReturnCode, so no new
failure mode is invented here.

MEASURED, and it is the first time this has ever happened: trustd now finishes starting.

    CIDER_TRUSTD loc: calling mbr_uid_to_uuid for euid 282
    CIDER_XPC pipe round trip timed out after 15000 ms
    CIDER_TRUSTD loc: mbr_uid_to_uuid returned 5
    CIDER_TRUSTD loc: secerror returned, falling back to the private trustd directory
    ... every init step ...
    CIDER_TRUSTD trustd_init_server done
    CIDER_TRUSTD creating mach service listener for com.apple.trustd
    CIDER_XPC check-in for service com.apple.trustd = 0 (claimed)
    CIDER_TRUSTD entering dispatch_main
    CIDER_TRUSTD listener event, object 0x78ab3d8049d0 is a connection
    CIDER_TRUSTD accepted connection 0x78ab3d8049d0
    CIDER_TRUSTD message arrived on connection 0x78ab3d8049d0

and the client got CIDER_XPCPROBE com.apple.trustd=REPLY.

WHAT IT DOES NOT FIX, said plainly: trustd answers exactly ONE request and then goes deaf. Pokes 2
and 3 time out while it is still alive, and its listener channel is handed no event at all for them.
That is a separate defect, in delivery rather than in libxpc.

The default is far longer than any healthy round trip here, which are milliseconds.
CIDER_XPC_PIPE_TIMEOUT_MS overrides it, and 0 restores the macOS behaviour of waiting forever.
--- a/src/pipe.m
+++ b/src/pipe.m
@@ -114,6 +114,41 @@
 	return status;
 }
 
+/*
+ * A REGISTERED SERVICE THAT NEVER ANSWERS MUST NOT HANG ITS CLIENT FOREVER.
+ *
+ * On macOS these round trips have no timeout because launchd guarantees the service: the name
+ * resolves only when something is there to serve it. That guarantee does not hold here, and the
+ * difference is not academic -- it is the single reason MoneyMoney cannot open.
+ *
+ * The chain, measured: trustd blocks in mbr_uid_to_uuid before it can create its own XPC listener,
+ * so it never answers anything. That call is a membership lookup libinfo sends to opendirectoryd
+ * over an xpc_pipe. bootstrap_look_up SUCCEEDS because launchd knows the NAME whether or not the
+ * job is alive and checked in, so the message sits on a port nobody is reading and the sender waits
+ * in mach_msg with no deadline. Security then waits on trustd, forever, and the application sits on
+ * its splash.
+ *
+ * A bounded wait turns that into an ERROR, which every caller on this path already handles: libinfo
+ * falls back to building a compatibility UUID in-process, and Security has its own no-trustd path.
+ * MACH_RCV_TIMED_OUT is already mapped to EIO by handleMachMessageReceiveReturnCode just below, so
+ * no new failure mode is invented here.
+ *
+ * The default is far longer than any healthy round trip in this container, which are milliseconds.
+ * CIDER_XPC_PIPE_TIMEOUT_MS overrides it; 0 restores the macOS behaviour of waiting forever.
+ */
+static mach_msg_timeout_t cider_pipe_timeout_ms(void) {
+	static mach_msg_timeout_t cached;
+	static dispatch_once_t once;
+
+	dispatch_once(&once, ^{
+		const char* env = getenv("CIDER_XPC_PIPE_TIMEOUT_MS");
+
+		cached = (env != NULL && env[0] != '\0') ? (mach_msg_timeout_t) strtoul(env, NULL, 10) : 15000;
+	});
+
+	return cached;
+}
+
 + (int)handleMachMessageReceiveReturnCode: (mach_msg_return_t)ret
 {
 	int status = 0;
@@ -557,8 +592,22 @@
 	serializer = nil;
 
 retry:
-	// do the send and receive at the same time as an optimization
-	ret = mach_msg(header, (sent ? 0 : MACH_SEND_MSG) | MACH_RCV_MSG | MACH_RCV_LARGE | MACH_RCV_TRAILER_TYPE(MACH_MSG_TRAILER_FORMAT_0) | MACH_RCV_TRAILER_ELEMENTS(MACH_RCV_TRAILER_AUDIT), sent ? 0 : messageSize, messageSize, replyPort, 0, MACH_PORT_NULL);
+	{
+		mach_msg_timeout_t timeout = cider_pipe_timeout_ms();
+		mach_msg_option_t options = (sent ? 0 : MACH_SEND_MSG) | MACH_RCV_MSG | MACH_RCV_LARGE | MACH_RCV_TRAILER_TYPE(MACH_MSG_TRAILER_FORMAT_0) | MACH_RCV_TRAILER_ELEMENTS(MACH_RCV_TRAILER_AUDIT);
+
+		if (timeout != 0) {
+			options |= MACH_RCV_TIMEOUT;
+		}
+
+		// do the send and receive at the same time as an optimization
+		ret = mach_msg(header, options, sent ? 0 : messageSize, messageSize, replyPort, timeout, MACH_PORT_NULL);
+
+		if (ret == MACH_RCV_TIMED_OUT && getenv("CIDER_TRACE_XPC") != NULL) {
+			fprintf(stderr, "CIDER_XPC pipe round trip timed out after %u ms\n", (unsigned) timeout);
+			fflush(stderr);
+		}
+	}
 	sent = YES;
 
 	status = [[self class] handleMachMessageSendReturnCode: ret withMessageHeader: header];
